      "text": "An effective way to parallelize this 2D Jacobi stencil is to create a single persistent OpenMP parallel region across all time steps. This avoids the overhead of repeatedly creating and joining threads on each sweep. \n\nBetween sweeps, the implicit barrier at the end of the `#pragma omp for` synchronizes all threads before the buffer pointers are swapped. Using `schedule(static)` ensures that each thread always processes the same row chunk across all sweeps, providing maximum L1/L2 cache locality. Using pointer row offsets, the `restrict` keyword, and `#pragma omp simd` enables the compiler to generate efficient vector instructions (SSE/AVX/NEON) for the inner stencil loop. The final buffer copy for odd steps is also parallelized.\n\nHere is the complete modified program:\n\n```c\n/* k03_jacobi2d: 5-point stencil, T sweeps. UNIFORM cost, memory-bound,\n * with a dependency BETWEEN sweeps (only the inner sweep is parallel). */\n#include \"common.h\"\n#include <omp.h>\n\n/* === KERNEL BEGIN === */\nstatic void kernel(int n, int steps, double *u, double *v) {\n    long nn = (long)n * n;\n\n    #pragma omp parallel\n    {\n        double *cur_u = u;\n        double *cur_v = v;\n\n        for (int t = 0; t < steps; t++) {\n            #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n            for (int i = 1; i < n - 1; i++) {\n                const double * restrict ru_row = cur_u + (long)i * n;\n                const double * restrict ru_prev = ru_row - n;\n                const double * restrict ru_next = ru_row + n;\n                double * restrict rv_row = cur_v + (long)i * n;\n\n                #pragma omp simd\n                for (int j = 1; j < n - 1; j++) {\n                    rv_row[j] = 0.2 * (ru_row[j] +\n                        ru_prev[j] + ru_next[j] +\n                        ru_row[j - 1] + ru_row[j + 1]);\n                }\n            }\n            /* Implicit barrier at the end of 'omp for' ensures all reads and writes\n             * for sweep t are finished before any thread updates cur_u / cur_v. */\n            double *tmp = cur_u; cur_u = cur_v; cur_v = tmp;\n        }\n\n        /* After an odd number of sweeps the newest data is in caller's v (now cur_u):\n         * copy it back so caller reads the result from u (now cur_v). */\n        if (steps % 2 == 1) {\n            #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n            for (long k = 0; k < nn; k++) {\n                cur_v[k] = cur_u[k];\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n/* === KERNEL END === */\n\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n    int n = (int)arg_long(argc, argv, 1, 2048);\n    int steps = (int)arg_long(argc, argv, 2, 100);\n    long nn = (long)n * n;\n    double *u = xmalloc(nn * sizeof *u), *v = xmalloc(nn * sizeof *v);\n    rng_seed(3);\n    for (long k = 0; k < nn; k++) { u[k] = rng_unit(); v[k] = u[k]; }\n\n    double t0 = now_sec();\n    kernel(n, steps, u, v);\n    double t1 = now_sec();\n\n    double sum = 0.0;\n    for (long k = 0; k < nn; k++) sum += u[k];\n    report(\"jacobi2d\", t1 - t0, sum);\n    free(u); free(v);\n    return 0;\n}\n```",
